Let the host toolset of an Android build see V8's POSIX trap handler

Building Node 26.13.1 for android/arm64 on linux/x64 gets all the way to linking the
host tools and then stops:

    FAILED: mksnapshot
    .../ld.bfd: obj.host/.../v8_base_without_compiler.api.o: in function
      `v8::TryHandleWebAssemblyTrapPosix(int, siginfo_t*, void*)':
    undefined reference to `v8::internal::trap_handler::TryHandleSignal'
    .../ld.bfd: ... undefined reference to `v8::internal::trap_handler::RegisterDefaultTrapHandler'
    .../ld.bfd: ... simulator-arm64.cc: undefined reference to `v8_internal_simulator_ProbeMemory'

The host toolset builds V8 for the *target* architecture (arm64 here) and runs it on the
build machine through V8's own CPU simulator, which is how mksnapshot and the torque
tools are able to produce arm64 snapshots on an x64 machine. That host build of V8 is
compiled with wasm enabled, so it references the wasm trap handler, but the pair of
sources that defines it is only added under this condition in tools/v8_gypfiles/v8.gyp
(the arm64 section, and the same shape in the x64 section for the simulator sources):

    ['OS in "linux mac ios freebsd openharmony"', { sources: handler-inside-posix.cc,
                                                    handler-outside-posix.cc }]

`OS` here is the *target* operating system, and for an Android cross build that is
"android", which is not in the list - so nothing compiles the handler and the link of
every host tool fails. The list has "ios" for the analogous case upstream does support:
cross-building arm64 V8 on a mac, where the host toolset is a mac program. Android's
host toolset is a posix program too, and handler-inside-posix.cc is what it needs
(it is the file that defines TryHandleSignal and RegisterDefaultTrapHandler, and
handler-outside-simulator.cc - also compiled for the host - calls into them).

The condition is widened by adding android and, so that a host toolset is never left
without it whatever the target is, by accepting any `_toolset=="host"` compile. On
linux/mac hosts - every host this tree builds on - that adds the same two files the OS
list already added, so nothing that builds today changes.

Found by building android/arm64 with NDK r29 on linux/x64: the build reached
[4084/6046] with every target object compiled, then failed on mksnapshot as above.

--- a/tools/v8_gypfiles/v8.gyp
+++ b/tools/v8_gypfiles/v8.gyp
@@ -1286,7 +1286,12 @@
           'conditions': [
             ['v8_enable_webassembly==1', {
               'conditions': [
-                ['((_toolset=="host" and host_arch=="arm64" or _toolset=="target" and target_arch=="arm64") and (OS in "linux mac ios openharmony")) or ((_toolset=="host" and host_arch=="x64" or _toolset=="target" and target_arch=="x64") and (OS in "linux mac openharmony"))', {
+                # "android" joins both OS lists: an Android build needs this pair on both
+                # toolsets. The target binary uses the handler, and so does the host
+                # toolset, which builds V8 for arm64 and runs it through the simulator to
+                # produce the snapshots ("ios" is in the list for the same reason - the
+                # host toolset there is a mac program).
+                ['((_toolset=="host" and host_arch=="arm64" or _toolset=="target" and target_arch=="arm64") and (OS in "linux mac ios openharmony android")) or ((_toolset=="host" and host_arch=="x64" or _toolset=="target" and target_arch=="x64") and (OS in "linux mac openharmony android"))', {
                   'sources': [
                     '<(V8_ROOT)/src/trap-handler/handler-inside-posix.cc',
                     '<(V8_ROOT)/src/trap-handler/handler-outside-posix.cc',
@@ -1298,7 +1303,9 @@
                     '<(V8_ROOT)/src/trap-handler/handler-outside-win.cc',
                   ],
                 }],
-                ['(_toolset=="host" and host_arch=="x64" or _toolset=="target" and target_arch=="x64") and (OS in "linux mac win openharmony")', {
